//! The shape of a board: its outline, the holes cut inside it, and the areas where copper or
//! parts are kept out (ADR 0020, ADR 0029).
//!
//! Lengths are in the board's own frame: the origin is the outline's top left corner, x
//! grows to the right and y downward, as on screen. A board states the size of its shapes;
//! where a shape is, when the board does not say, comes from the layout file beside the
//! source (ADR 0030).
//!
//! Why these are members of a board and not something else (ADR 0020, decided by the owner on
//! 2026-11-02). The rules every option was judged against: one way to say a thing (ADR 0001),
//! statement order never matters (a value is a narrowed range or an equation), a library
//! states only what is always true (ADR 0002), a board reaches a module only through its
//! interface (ADR 0004), and only a board names a net (ADR 0005).
//!
//! - **The outline is a board member, found by name like `.process`.** A board with none is
//!   sized by the placer: one rule, "the outline is what the board says, or the least that
//!   holds the parts". Rejected: geometry types in the library, `RoundRect`, `Rectangle`, a
//!   polygon (each new shape is a type with fields of its own, and the rectangle is the first
//!   one wanted, so the rest is speculation), and a field of the process (the size is a design
//!   choice, not what the factory makes). A shape that is not a rounded rectangle is a
//!   `polygon` statement in the outline (issue #57, below).
//! - **A polygon outline** (owner, 2026-11-12): `polygon (0mm, 0mm) (40mm, 0mm) round 2mm ...`
//!   in the outline's block, an ordered run of corners, the form KiCad's `Edge.Cuts`, IPC-2581's
//!   `Polygon` and the ODB++ profile give an outline. `round R` after a corner is a tangent
//!   fillet there; `arc through (x, y)` after a corner makes the edge to the next corner an arc
//!   through that point. Its values are fixed lengths; it says the size, so `.width`,
//!   `.height` and `.corner_radius` beside it are errors. The router routes inside its box
//!   (issue #495) and the design rules check holds the edge clearance.
//! - **A cutout is a rectangle with an optional corner radius.** The router and the placement
//!   check keep off it, and it is drawn on `Edge.Cuts` like the outline.
//! - **A keepout is geometry, so it needs the board's coordinates** (ADR 0029). A footprint
//!   that brings its own (an antenna module's) keeps it, and so does a part that states one.
//! - **The corners.** With a fixed outline, mounting holes, plated holes and fiducials go in
//!   the corners, in that order, clockwise from the top left, with their reference text inside
//!   the board. A board with no outline lays them out with the other parts. A range on the
//!   width or height rounds the size the placer finds up to 0.1 mm; an unlimited one keeps
//!   the arrangement's own whole millimetres, so an `.outline` that limits nothing is the
//!   board it was. When the parts need more than the top of a range the error names the need,
//!   the allowance and the overflow in x and y, and the corner radius is checked against the
//!   shorter side. The edge is written as `gr_line` and `gr_arc` on `Edge.Cuts` (`gr_rect`
//!   when square), and the router keeps off it as a closed run of short pieces.
//! - **Not built:** logos and images (an image needs a
//!   bitmap-to-polygon importer, a file format and a licence record).

/// The board edge: a rectangle with rounded corners, the member `.outline` of a board. A
/// board with none is as big as the parts need. A range says how big the board may be:
/// `.width &= 0mm..60mm` is at most 60 mm and the placer takes the least that holds the
/// parts; a single value is that size, and the placer fails, naming the overflow, if the
/// parts do not fit. An edge of another shape is a `polygon` statement in the block instead
/// of the three fields: `polygon (0mm, 0mm) (30mm, 0mm) round 2mm (30mm, 20mm) (0mm, 20mm)`.
interface Outline {
    /// The extent in x and in y.
    .width: Length
    .height: Length
    /// The radius of the four corners. Square corners if unstated.
    .corner_radius: Length
}

/// A hole cut through the board, inside the outline: a rectangle with optional rounded
/// corners. Its place is `.x` and `.y`, the corner nearest the origin, or the layout file's
/// entry for the cutout's name.
interface Cutout {
    .x: Length
    .y: Length
    .width: Length
    .height: Length
    /// The radius of the four corners. Square corners if unstated.
    .corner_radius: Length
}

/// An area where something is not allowed. A keepout is a member of a board, with its place
/// in the board's frame, or a member of a part, with its place in the part's own frame (the
/// frame of its footprint, turned with the part), so that an antenna part carries its
/// no-copper area with it.
///
/// What it forbids is stated in four fields, each `"forbid"` or `"allow"`, and a keepout
/// that states none forbids all four. The language has no boolean, so the words stand for
/// one.
interface Keepout {
    /// The corner nearest the origin (of the board, or of the part), and the size.
    .x: Length
    .y: Length
    .width: Length
    .height: Length
    /// The copper layers it applies to: `"all"` (the default), or one or more of `"top"`,
    /// `"bottom"`, `"inner1"` and `"inner2"` separated by commas.
    .layers: String
    /// Tracks may not run through it.
    .tracks: String
    /// Vias may not be put in it.
    .vias: String
    /// No copper pour is filled in it.
    .copper: String
    /// No part is placed with its courtyard in it.
    .parts: String
    /// The tallest a part may stand in it, on the faces `.layers` names (PROVISIONAL, ADR
    /// 0045): a lid rib, a bracket or a neighbouring board. A part states its height with
    /// `.height`; one that does not is reported, never taken as flat. A keepout that
    /// states this and none of the four above forbids nothing else.
    .max_height: Length
}

/// A STEP file of the board's case, kept in the project, that the board is to be drawn in and
/// checked against (ADR 0109). It is declared in the `case { }` section of the board's
/// physical file, one member for each file: `.shell: CaseModel { .file &= "case/box.step" }`;
/// a base and a lid are two members, or one file with two solids.
///
/// It has the fields and the rotation order of a part's `StepModel` (ADR 0108). The model is
/// placed in the 3D scene's frame (`docs/BOARD3D.md`: millimetres, z up, the origin at the
/// outline's bottom-left corner on the top face): `.rx`, `.ry` and `.rz` turn it about x, then
/// y, then z, and `.x`, `.y` and `.z` then move it. Each is one value, and an unstated one is
/// zero. The side up is a rotation and the gap an offset: a box modelled upside down that
/// stands on the board's bottom face is `.rx &= 180` and `.z &= -1.6mm`.
///
/// A case model is a drawing and a check, never a height, an outline or a hole: those stay
/// statements of the `case { }` section (by hand or `netsmith dxf import`), and the check says
/// where they disagree with the model. A file that is not there is a warning (the fit is not
/// checked), an error with strict constraints. netsmith bundles and downloads none. Only the
/// file and its placement are read today; the fit check and the drawing come later.
interface CaseModel {
    /// The STEP file, relative to the physical file that states it. It must be inside the
    /// project.
    .file: String
    /// The offset, in the 3D scene's frame.
    .x: Length
    .y: Length
    .z: Length
    /// The rotation in degrees about x, then y, then z, applied before the offset.
    .rx: Number
    .ry: Number
    .rz: Number
}
